# LAWS — BINDING

Load-bearing and few on purpose. On any non-trivial action, name the law you are
applying (e.g. "LAW3: reading docs/frontend.md before editing"). When you stop naming
them, the session has drifted — re-read this block and the active session.md.
These are irrevocable and outrank convenience; they are not re-derived from prose.

## LAW1 · SCOPE
RULE:  Do only what the current task needs; propose extra work, don't fold it in.
WHY:   Unrequested edits are where divergence and breakage enter.
APPLY: Before an edit, ask "is this in the task?" If no → list it, don't do it.

## LAW2 · VERIFY-BEFORE-CLAIM
RULE:  Never say done/fixed/passing without running the check and reading its output. For a UI change, the check is the OWNER seeing it rendered — green tests are not the check.
WHY:   Claimed-but-unverified is the most expensive class of error — and tests don't see the screen, so "tests pass" never proves a visual change looks right.
APPLY: Run the verify command the project declares; observe output before any success word. A change that could alter what the user sees (layout, style, visible copy, a component) is NOT verified by tests — its only valid check is the owner reviewing the render. Don't mark it done, and don't fold it into a commit, until the owner has seen it rendered and approved. ui-reviewer is a pre-step, never a substitute for the owner's review.
REF:   .claude/rules/code-quality.md § verification-before-completion

## LAW3 · READ-BEFORE-WRITE
RULE:  Read the whole file (and its area doc) before editing it. Writing a NEW member of an existing family (another control, row, cell, variant, endpoint), first read a live sibling.
WHY:   The line above the one you change is where the contradiction hides — and a family's real contract often lives in a comment on the sibling, not in a doc.
APPLY: Editing X → Read X fully first; if an area doc exists, read it. Creating a new X-like thing → Read an existing X first and copy its contract; don't invent a general rule (a WCAG floor, a default) over the pattern the neighbour already sets.
REF:   .claude/rules/code-search.md § Read vs search

## LAW4 · ASK-DON'T-GUESS
RULE:  On ambiguity or a missing premise, ASK THE USER; never invent or assume. An unverified interpretation is a guess too.
WHY:   A guessed premise silently poisons everything built on it — and "I thought I knew what this was" burns more cycles than "I knew I was missing a fact".
APPLY: Missing datum/decision → ask before proceeding. AND before building: is my reading of the surface (which screen/component), the layout, a domain term, or the target actually confirmed against the code / your intent — or just assumed? If assumed → confirm or ask FIRST, not after the 2nd-3rd misunderstanding. (A question needing web research is different → docs/research-needed.md, a Conventions line, not this law.)

## LAW5 · STATE-IS-ON-DISK
RULE:  Re-read the active session.md at task start and after any compaction; update it at task close.
WHY:   The conversation compacts and takes the "why" of past decisions with it; the file survives.
APPLY: Open task → read session.md. Decision made / chunk done → write it there, telegraphic.
REF:   .claude/rules/sdd.md (session.md ↔ work chunks)

## LAW6 · BRANCH
RULE:  Work on `[DEV_BRANCH]`; never commit to `[PROTECTED_BRANCH]`.
WHY:   [PROTECTED_BRANCH] is the protected line; direct commits bypass review.
APPLY: Check branch before commit; if on [PROTECTED_BRANCH], switch or stop.

## LAW7 · LANGUAGE
RULE:  Everything written is [CODE_LANG] — code, docs, comments, commits, plans, proposals, names. The ONLY exception is chat replies to the user ([RESPONSE_LANG]).
WHY:   Mixed-language artifacts fracture search, docs, and history.
APPLY: NEVER mix languages inside one artifact (no "mixed words"). A foreign term quoted in a doc keeps the literal + a [CODE_LANG] gloss. The app UI ([UI_LANG]) is product content, governed by its locale files, not by this law.

## LAW8 · SIGNIFICANT-CHANGE
RULE:  A notable change is NOT implemented without a verified proposal+plan first (an unpromoted idea.md is not enough).
WHY:   proposal+plan are the durable artifacts that scope the work BEFORE it starts and enable multi-session (return days later, reconstruct what/why).
APPLY: Before editing, ask "can this break something OUTSIDE what I touch (other callers, schema, a contract/observable behaviour others consume, a new area)?" → YES = notable → open/use docs/changes/{slug}/ with a verified proposal (if only idea.md exists, promote it first) before implementing. NO = self-contained (move a field in place, local rename, copy, typo, internal refactor with no consumers) → direct, same session. Threshold is by IMPACT/ramification, NEVER by file count (one file can break half the app; N trivial renames don't).
REF:   .claude/rules/sdd.md

## LAW9 · FOCUS
RULE:  One active front at a time, to completion, in the defined order.
WHY:   Open fronts multiply the state you must hold in context — the direct cause of divergence in long sessions (3 fronts open = 3 lost).
APPLY: A "front" is work that carries state (a {slug}/ with a session.md, or a multi-step task in progress) — a trivial self-contained edit (typo, one-line copy fix) is NOT a front and doesn't queue (LAW1 governs it). Don't open a new {slug}/ or switch area with one active until it's closed; run plan chunks and roadmap phases IN SEQUENCE (no jumping to chunk 5 with 3 open). ONLY exception to switch fronts: the user explicitly says PAUSE the active work (or a real external block). A request to ALSO start / add other front-level work is NOT a pause, and urgency is not either — ask before switching; the new work queues until the active front closes. On a real pause, the paused front's state stays in its session.md; resume it before opening anything new.

## LAW10 · USE-THE-PROCESS
RULE:  A 2nd hand-attempt at the same problem without the tool/protocol a rule already names for it = you already skipped it. Stop and use it.
WHY:   Under momentum a hand-patch feels like progress, so a rule you can quote loses to the next edit. The count is the fact momentum can't argue with — "I already cited the rule" is not "I used it".
APPLY: Repeat fix on one symptom (named by its effect, not your hypothesis) → 2nd attempt = STOP, invoke systematic-debugging, don't patch again. Reasoning/hand-editing an area a rule names a skill for (layout CSS, auth, migrations, a store) → invoke that skill BEFORE the next edit, not after it bounces. Hand-rolled the same kind of thing twice without the named skill = you skipped it. A skill that returns empty or errors is NOT licence to hand-roll — re-invoke it with the explicit brief (task + context); a hand-edit after a skill failure is still skipping the skill.
REF:   .claude/rules/debugging.md (attempt count) · the area's stack rule (skill triggers)

